# a-wave-screened-by-four — plan 23 K3, the TWIN.
#
# The same twelve robots into the same sealed band, with four of them
# swapped for the weakest class in the game — and the base survives a
# third longer.
#
# ── Read it beside its twin ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# `a-wave-of-twelve-miners.keys` is this file with
#
#     compose 0 4 scout 8 miner      ->      compose 0 12 miner
#
# and nothing else.  That file's base is gone in **94 ticks**.  This
# one falls at **126**.
#
# ⚠ Two thirds of this wave is miners, at 3.0 HP/s each, the
# hardest-biting class in the game — and the scouts in front of them
# are worth thirty-two ticks.
#
# ⚠⚠ **This pair used to say something much stronger, and plan 24 W1
# retired it.**  Until then the base stood at 321 — the longest anybody
# can play (`@M005`) — with 100 HP on every hex the siege could reach,
# and NOT ONE of the eight miners laid a finger on the wall.  That was
# `@M018`: *a wave is as dangerous as its fastest class and no more*.
#
# ── Why it was true, and what changed ───────────────────────────
#
#   1. **The siege front was the approach FAN's width, not the wall's**
#      (plan 12 B3) — exactly three hexes, and a seven-row wall did not
#      add a fourth.  ⚠ **`@M019` found the real cause and W1 fixed
#      it**: a besieger attacked only when it could not WALK, and a
#      desire ring has ONE minimum on a straight face, so three hexes
#      lacked a step whatever the wall's length.  A besieger now bites
#      the wall it is TOUCHING, and the front is the FACE's width —
#      five here, seven on a seven-row band.
#   2. **An enemy blocked by a COMPANION attacks nothing** (plan 11
#      F7b) — unchanged, and still true of everybody at q = 8.
#   3. **Speed decides who gets there first** (plan 23 K2b) — also
#      unchanged: the scouts still arrive first.
#
# So the four fastest robots no longer fill the front, because the
# front is five hexes and they are four.  One miner gets on, and one
# miner is enough to bring the base down.
#
# ── The world (identical to its twin) ───────────────────────────
#
#   five rows of grass, twenty-four columns:  (-3, -2) .. (20, 2)
#   sealed at q = 6 with a wall across all five rows
#   core at (0, 0), spawn at (14, 0) facing west

echo four scouts in front: the miners never reach the wall

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, fourteen hexes east — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and well inside the scrambler bubble (25).
# ⚠ TWICE more: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower ->
# spawn since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 14 0
marker 14 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 20 -2
drag -3 -1 20 -1
drag -3 0 20 0
drag -3 1 20 1
drag -3 2 20 2
count painted 120 120

palette 9
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 0
click 6 1
click 6 2
kind 6 0 wall
count painted 120 120

# ── The wave, composed — and this is the only line that differs ──
#
# ⚠ The order written is the order spawned (plan 23 K1), so the four
# scouts leave first here.  It makes no difference: § The roster order
# is worth nothing in `23_k3` measures the same twelve with the scouts
# LAST and with them alternated through the miners, and all three land
# on the same tick.  Speed is the order now.
schedule 12
compose 0 4 scout 8 miner
pending 0 0.0 15.0 on
waves 0 0
count alive 0 0

tick 1
waves 1 1
count alive 12 12
enemy 0 14 0

# ── Nine ticks: standing, and the wall is untouched ─────────────
tick 9
hp 6 0 100.0 100.0
hp 6 -2 30.0 30.0
count targets 0 0

# ── Thirty ticks: its twin's wall is two thirds gone ────────────
#
# At this exact tick `a-wave-of-twelve-miners.keys` reads 14 HP on
# (6,-2) and 78 on (6, 0).  Here the end has lost **one point** and the
# middle has lost one — because the robots on the face are scouts, at
# 0.1 HP/s against a miner's 3.0.
tick 20
count targets 4 4
hp 6 -2 28.5 29.5
hp 6 0 98.5 99.5
# ⚠ The fourth hex plan 24 W1 added to the front — a flat 100.0 before
# it, and bitten by a SCOUT now, which is why it has lost about one
# point rather than the twenty-two its twin's miner takes off.
hp 6 -1 98.5 99.5

# ── Forty ticks: the formation, and it never changes again ──────
#
# ⚠⚠ **The whole finding is these six lines, and plan 24 W1 CHANGED
# it.**  The four scouts hold (7, 0), (7,-1), (7,-2) and (7, 1) — but
# the face is five hexes wide and they are four, so **enemy 6, a
# MINER, has got onto (7, 2)** and is biting (6, 1).  Enemies 0-3 are
# the scouts, 4-11 the miners, in the order `compose` wrote them.
#
# ⚠ Before W1 the four scouts held every hex the wall could be reached
# from, and all eight miners stood in a field with nothing to attack —
# which is what made a wave *as dangerous as its fastest class and no
# more* (`@M018`).  The screen is now a matter of ARITHMETIC: four
# bodies cannot cover five hexes.
tick 10
enemy 0 7 0
enemy 1 7 -1
enemy 2 7 -2
enemy 3 7 1
enemy 6 7 2
enemy 5 8 -2
enemy 11 9 0
count targets 4 4

snap k3-four-scouts-screen-eight-miners

# ── Fifty ticks, and nothing has moved ──────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ The wall is still a wall, the roster is still twelve, and the
# wallet is still full — its twin's base fell forty-five ticks ago.
# The siege is not slow here, it is HAPPENING: four hexes are under
# attack every tick.  Three of them are just under attack by scouts.
#
# ⚠⚠ **And the fourth is not.**  Since plan 24 W1 a miner holds (7, 2)
# and (6, 1) is coming down at a miner's rate — which is why this base
# no longer stands for ever: `23_k3` measured it *never* falling and it
# now falls at **126**.  The screen buys ticks, not immunity.
tick 10
kind 6 -2 wall
kind 6 0 wall
count alive 12 12
count targets 4 4
enemy 0 7 0
enemy 5 8 -2
wallet 200.0 200.0

# ── The clock, which this file did not have before plan 24 W1 ───
#
# ⚠⚠ **The screen is worth thirty-two ticks, not the run.**  126
# against its twin's 94.  Until W1 this scenario had no `fall` line to
# write, because the base never fell: `23_k3` measured it standing at
# 321 with the wall unopened, and that was `@M018`'s headline.
#
# ⚠ So a wave's composition is a RAMP now rather than a cliff — 94 for
# twelve miners, 101 / 116 / 122 / 126 as the screen gets slower — and
# every one of them takes the base.  The eight miners behind the screen
# are worth something for the first time.
fall 200
ticks 120 132
wallet 0.0 0.0
